<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rai people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rai are a group of Tibeto-Burman-speaking Kiranti peoples of the hills of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, with communities in Sikkim and Darjeeling. They speak more than twenty related languages, among them Bantawa, Chamling and Kulung. Their religious traditions, based on ancestor rites and oral texts, have much in common with those of the Limbu, and like them they regard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirata">Kirata</a> kings of Nepal's chronicles as their ancestors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rai-people.jpg" alt="Rai people" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raibisu43 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kirat_Rai_Shaman_From_Himalayas_of_nepal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Rai, also called Khambu or Jimee, belong to the Kirat family of peoples. Their oral tradition, the Mundum, tells that the first Kirati ancestors came out of Khuwalung and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>'s eastern hills through the Barahachhetra gorge of the Kosi valley, then travelled up the Arun, Dudh Koshi, Sun Koshi and Tama Koshi rivers. According to the genealogies, between 29 and 32 Kirati kings ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> valley for many centuries, beginning with their chieftain Yalambar, until the Lichhavi dynasty defeated the last of them. The Kirati then moved east, and the groups that followed different rivers became the Rai, Yakkha, Sunuwar and Limbu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the eastern hills the Kirati ruled small territories for centuries, later divided into Wallo Kirat, Majh Kirat and Pallo Kirat. From about 1743 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> of Gorkha began the campaign that united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. When he invaded the Dhulikhel area in 1768, the Kirat king Chaukhat Mahindra Sing Rai and his brother Namsing Rai resisted for a long time, but Kirati rule in the eastern hills ended with the Gorkha conquest of 1772 to 1773.</p><p>The Rai number about 750,000 in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, mainly in districts such as Khotang, Bhojpur, Solukhambu, Sankhuwasabha and Dhankuta. They are the largest single ethnic community in Sikkim, live in Darjeeling, Kalimpong, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and Bhutan, and some have recently moved to the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Each subtribe, or thar, has its own language or dialect, and is divided into exogamous clans called pacha. A married woman keeps her own clan while her children take their father's. Most Rai are farmers, growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, wheat and mustard on dry terraces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> in wet fields.</p><p>Their religion rests on nature and ancestor worship, led by shamans such as the nakchong and bijuwa, with no religious hierarchy. Sumnima, the earth and ancestral mother, and Paruhang, the sky god, are their ancestral deities. Every house keeps Suptulung, the three hearth stones at the centre of every rite from birth to death. In the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> about 65 per cent followed Kirat Mundhum and 30 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. Sakela, danced at the full moons of Baisakh and Mangsir, is the best-known festival.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rai people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name links them to the Kiranti peoples of eastern Nepal, such as the <b>Rai</b> and Limbu, who regard the dynasty as their ancestors.</blockquote><a href="/state/kirata-dynasty">Kirata dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
